{"name":"Quagmire Green","hex":"#998811","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"quagmire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A swampy, soggy area of ground.","A perilous, mixed up and troubled situation; a hopeless tangle."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To embroil (a person, etc.) in complexity or difficulty."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/quagmire"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Peatland","blocks":[{"text":"A peatland is a type of wetland whose soils consist of organic matter from decaying plants, forming layers of peat. Peatlands arise because of incomplete decomposition of organic matter, usually litter from vegetation, due to water-logging and subsequent anoxia. Peatlands are unusual landforms that derive mostly from biological rather than physical processes, and can take on characteristic shapes and surface patterning."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg/330px-ARG-2016-Aerial-Tierra_del_Fuego_%28Ushuaia%29%E2%80%93Valle_Carbajal_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peatland"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Avaste_soo_p%C3%B5hjaosa_vaated_%283%29.JPG/500px-Avaste_soo_p%C3%B5hjaosa_vaated_%283%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Avaste_soo_p%C3%B5hjaosa_vaated_%283%29.JPG/1280px-Avaste_soo_p%C3%B5hjaosa_vaated_%283%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Avaste_soo_p%C3%B5hjaosa_vaated_%283%29.JPG/500px-Avaste_soo_p%C3%B5hjaosa_vaated_%283%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Avaste Fen, one of the largest fens in Estonia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAvaste_soo_p%C3%B5hjaosa_vaated_(3).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Ruwenzori_Upper_Bigo_Bog.jpg/500px-Ruwenzori_Upper_Bigo_Bog.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Ruwenzori_Upper_Bigo_Bog.jpg/1280px-Ruwenzori_Upper_Bigo_Bog.jpg?utm_so